<section id="what-to-look-for" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="what-to-look-for">What to look for</h2>
<p>The demonstration deliberately separates three elements:</p>
<ol type="1">
<li><strong>The baseline</strong> represents movement that occurs throughout the day.</li>
<li><strong>Two Gaussian peaks</strong> represent morning and evening demand.</li>
<li><strong>The control</strong> applies the same multiplier to both peaks, making the assumption visible and easy to challenge.</li>
</ol>
<p>Changing mode swaps in a different set of synthetic parameters. Walking has a flatter profile; bus travel has stronger commute peaks; cycling sits between them. These are illustrative shapes only, not empirical estimates.</p>
</section>
<section id="reproduce-or-adapt-it" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="reproduce-or-adapt-it">Reproduce or adapt it</h2>
<p>The complete calculation is in the code cell above. A real analysis could replace <code>modeSettings</code> with a CSV file, an API response or the output of an R/Python data pipeline. Quarto can then keep the narrative, citations, code and interactive result together in one page.</p>
<p>For a production analysis I would also record:</p>
<ul>
<li>the source, licence and retrieval date of every input;</li>
<li>cleaning and exclusion decisions;</li>
<li>the software environment or lockfile;</li>
<li>uncertainty and sensitivity checks; and</li>
<li>a stable snapshot of any source that changes over time.</li>
</ul>
<p>That is the pattern this site is set up to support.</p>


