<item>
  <title>A practical pattern for reproducible vignettes</title>
  <dc:creator>James Hulse</dc:creator>
  <link>https://hulsiejames.github.io/articles/reproducible-vignette-pattern/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 




<p>Reproducibility is not achieved by adding a repository link at the end of an article. It starts with the way the analysis is organised and explained.</p>
<p>This is the default pattern I plan to use for longer technical pieces on this site.</p>
<section id="begin-with-a-precise-question" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="begin-with-a-precise-question">1. Begin with a precise question</h2>
<p>State what the analysis is trying to learn before introducing tools or data. A reader should be able to tell what would count as an answer—and what falls outside the scope.</p>
</section>
<section id="describe-the-evidence" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="describe-the-evidence">2. Describe the evidence</h2>
<p>Record each input’s source, coverage, retrieval date and licence. If a dataset changes over time, keep a stable snapshot or an exact version identifier.</p>
<table class="caption-top table">
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Field</th>
<th>What to record</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Source</td>
<td>Publisher and canonical URL</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Coverage</td>
<td>Geography, dates and population</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Version</td>
<td>Release, retrieval date or checksum</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Licence</td>
<td>Reuse terms and attribution</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Known limits</td>
<td>Gaps, biases and measurement issues</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section id="keep-transformations-visible" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="keep-transformations-visible">3. Keep transformations visible</h2>
<p>Code should sit close to the claim it supports. Long setup sections can be folded by default, while decisive filters, joins and model choices remain easy to inspect.</p>
<div class="code-copy-outer-scaffold"><div class="sourceCode" id="cb1" style="background: #f1f3f5;"><pre class="sourceCode r code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode r"><span id="cb1-1"><span class="co" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;"># A deliberately small example of an auditable transformation</span></span>
<span id="cb1-2">daily_counts <span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">|&gt;</span></span>
<span id="cb1-3">  dplyr<span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">::</span><span class="fu" style="color: #4758AB;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">filter</span>(<span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">!</span><span class="fu" style="color: #4758AB;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">is.na</span>(mode)) <span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">|&gt;</span></span>
<span id="cb1-4">  dplyr<span class="sc" style="color: #5E5E5E;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">::</span><span class="fu" style="color: #4758AB;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">count</span>(date, mode, <span class="at" style="color: #657422;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">wt =</span> trips, <span class="at" style="color: #657422;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">name =</span> <span class="st" style="color: #20794D;
background-color: null;
font-style: inherit;">"trips"</span>)</span></code></pre></div></div>
</section>
<section id="put-interpretation-beside-output" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="put-interpretation-beside-output">4. Put interpretation beside output</h2>
<p>A chart does not explain itself. Describe the dominant pattern, the comparison that matters and the uncertainty that should temper the conclusion. Interactive controls should reveal a meaningful choice—not decorate the page.</p>
</section>
<section id="make-rerunning-realistic" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="make-rerunning-realistic">5. Make rerunning realistic</h2>
<p>For R or Python work, pin dependencies with <code>renv</code> or a Python environment file. Quarto’s frozen computations can preserve article outputs between runs, while the environment files explain how to recreate them.</p>
<div class="callout callout-style-default callout-note callout-titled">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-title-container flex-fill">
Note
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p>This site uses <code>freeze: auto</code>. When a computational article is rendered locally, its results can be committed in <code>_freeze/</code>; the publishing action can then rebuild the website without rerunning every historical analysis.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="finish-with-boundaries" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="finish-with-boundaries">6. Finish with boundaries</h2>
<p>Say what the analysis cannot establish, what would change the conclusion, and what should be tried next. That final section is often more useful than a polished summary.</p>
<p>The template in this repository turns these headings into a practical starting point for the next vignette.</p>


